cards.body:
Three complete programs that hand Jet closures to C (D-FFI-CONCUR1). Each callback parameter carries a retention
fact: `.CallScoped` (used only during the call), `.UntilClose` (kept until a release call) or `.Forever`. The
retention decides what a closure may capture. A retained callback returns a `Close` registration that unregisters
at scope end. Callback types are infallible, and a panic never unwinds through C. Outputs are design targets.

## Simple: qsort with a capturing comparator

```jet
use libc: c.stdlib

Person: struct {
    name: String
    age: Int
}

run: fn() {
    people := [Person{name: "Ann", age: 41}, Person{name: "Bo", age: 29}, Person{name: "Cy", age: 35}]
    oldest_first := true                                    // a local the comparator reads
    libc.qsort(people, (a, b) -> {                         // qsort: noescape -> call-scoped
        if oldest_first -> I32.from_int(b.age - a.age) else -> I32.from_int(a.age - b.age)
    })
    loop p in people -> print(p.name)
}
```

```text
Ann
Cy
Bo
```

The `cbind` row for `qsort` is `fn qsort<T>(base: &[T], compar: fn(T, T) -> I32)`: the `base`, `nmemb` and `size`
parameters collapse into one write window because the libc pack states their relation. The comparator becomes a
generated trampoline with a context pointer, one indirect call per comparison, as in C.

## Medium: an event loop (libuv timers and signals)

```jet
package: {
    name: "ticker"
    deps: { uv: c@nixpkgs:libuv }
}

use uv: c.uv

run: fn() {
    lp: uv.Loop.new()                                     // uv_loop_init / uv_loop_close as Close
    ticks := 0
    timer: lp.timer()                                     // uv_timer_t handle, Close via uv_close
    _t: timer.start(100ms, repeat: 100ms, (t) -> {        // .UntilClose; runs on the loop's thread (this one)
        ticks += 1                                          // allowed: thread .Caller, the loop runs on this task
        print("tick {ticks}")
        if ticks == 3 -> t.stop()
    })
    _s: lp.signal(.Int, (s) -> {
        print("interrupted")
        lp.stop()
    })
    lp.run(.Default)                                        // a blocking row on this task
    print("done after {ticks} ticks")
}                                                           // _s, _t, timer, lp close in reverse order
```

```text
tick 1
tick 2
tick 3
done after 3 ticks
```

Why `ticks += 1` is allowed in a retained callback: the pack states that libuv calls the timer callback only from
`uv_run` on the loop's own thread (`thread .Caller`), and the registration closes before `ticks` goes out of scope.
So the capture is a borrow that provably outlives every call. Without the thread fact, the capture would be
refused with E3233 and the fix "make it `shared`".

## Complex: callbacks from a foreign thread

What it shows: an audio library that calls back from its real-time thread; one closure delivered on the owning
task, one running directly on the foreign thread with only sendable captures; a panic that stops without
unwinding through C.

```jet
package: {
    name: "meter"
    deps: { audio: c@nixpkgs:miniaudio }
}

use ma: c.miniaudio

Level: struct {
    peak: F32
    frames: Int
}

run: fn() {
    device: ma.Device.open_capture(channels: 1, sample_rate: 48000)
    level: shared Level{peak: 0.0, frames: 0}

    // 1. Runs on the audio thread: must be fast, captures only sendable values.
    _fast: device.on_data((input) -> {                    // .UntilClose, thread .Foreign
        p: input.samples().map((s) -> s.abs()).max() ?? 0.0
        level.peak = p
        level.frames += input.frame_count()
    })

    // 2. Delivered on this task: may touch task-local state, may wait.
    history := [F32]{}
    _slow: device.on_notification((n) -> {
        history.push(level.peak)
        print("device {n.kind}: peak so far {level.peak}")
    }, deliver: .Owner)

    device.start()
    loop i in 0..<10 {
        sleep(100ms)
        print("{i}: peak {level.peak} after {level.frames} frames")
    }
    device.stop()
    print("history: {history.len()} notifications")
}
```

```text
0: peak 0.21 after 4800 frames
...
9: peak 0.37 after 48000 frames
history: 2 notifications
```

If closure 1 tried to append to `history`, the build would stop:

```text
Error [E3233]: `on_data` calls this callback on a foreign thread, so it cannot use `history`, a task-local list.
 Fix: Use `deliver: .Owner` to run the callback on this task, or make the value `shared`.
```

If closure 1 panicked, the stop report would show the Jet frame, the miniaudio frame and the crossing, and the
program would stop without unwinding through miniaudio's thread.

### Best competitor equivalent

```rust
// Rust: retained closures need Box<dyn FnMut + Send + 'static>, a trampoline, and manual unregistration.
extern "C" fn trampoline(dev: *mut ma_device, out: *mut c_void, inp: *const c_void, n: u32) {
    let cb = unsafe { &mut *((*dev).pUserData as *mut Box<dyn FnMut(&[f32]) + Send>) };
    cb(unsafe { std::slice::from_raw_parts(inp as *const f32, n as usize) });
}
// Panics must be caught with catch_unwind in every trampoline, or the process aborts.
```

Three complete programs that call C++ through `use cpp.<lib>`. The `cppbind` package (root `cpp`) reads headers
with pinned clang's JSON AST, writes a small C shim per used member, and emits Jet types. Constructors and
destructors become construction and `Close`; exceptions become Jet errors; templates are instantiated for the types
the program uses. Outputs are design targets. Design: `ffi-v3.md` sections 4 and 8.2; ballots D-FFI-LANG1,
D-FFI-FACTS1, D-FFI-CONCUR1.

## Simple: a C++ class

```cpp
// inventory.hpp, part of the project
#pragma once
#include <string>
#include <vector>
class Inventory {
public:
    explicit Inventory(std::string owner);
    void add(const std::string& item, int count);
    int count(const std::string& item) const;
    std::vector<std::string> items() const;
};
```

```jet
package: {
    name: "shop"
    deps: { inv: cpp@"src/inventory.hpp" }
}

use inv: cpp.inventory

run: fn() {
    store := inv.Inventory.new("Ada")          // constructor; the destructor runs at scope end
    store.add("apple", 3)                     // non-const member: writes; the call stays plain
    store.add("pear", 2)
    print(store.count("apple"))                // const member: plain read
    print(store.items())                       // std::vector<std::string> copied to [String]
}
```

```text
3
["apple", "pear"]
```

## Medium: templates and exceptions

What it shows: a template instantiated for the types the program uses; `noexcept(false)` members whose exception
types become a Jet error enum; `std::string_view` returned as a view tied to its owner.

```cpp
// matrix.hpp
template <typename T> class Matrix {
public:
    Matrix(size_t rows, size_t cols);
    T& at(size_t r, size_t c);                          // throws std::out_of_range
    Matrix<T> multiply(const Matrix<T>& other) const;   // throws std::invalid_argument
    std::string_view name() const noexcept;
};
```

```jet
package: {
    name: "linalg"
    deps: { mx: cpp@"src/matrix.hpp" }
}

use mx: cpp.matrix

product: fn() -> mx.Matrix<F64> !mx.CppError {
    a := mx.Matrix<F64>.new(2, 3)                       // instantiates Matrix<double>
    b := mx.Matrix<F64>.new(3, 2)
    loop r in 0..<2 {
        loop c in 0..<3 {
            a.set_at(r, c, Float.from_int(r + c))         // T& at() becomes get_at/set_at
            b.set_at(c, r, 1.0)
        }
    }
    a.multiply(b)                                       // std::invalid_argument -> CppError.InvalidArgument
}

run: fn() {
    p: product()
    print(p.get_at(1, 1))
    print(p.name())                                     // a view, valid while p lives
    bad: mx.Matrix<F64>.new(2, 2).get_at(5, 5)        // std::out_of_range -> CppError.OutOfRange
    print(bad)
}
```

```text
6.0
matrix
Error [CppError.OutOfRange]: Matrix::at: index (5, 5) outside 2x2
  in C++  matrix.hpp:14   Matrix<double>::at(size_t, size_t)
  -- crossing: cpp.matrix Matrix<F64>.get_at  (binder cppbind 2.1.0, rule emit_method) --
  in Jet  linalg.jet:24:12  run()
```

## Complex: a physics engine with callbacks

What it shows: a C++ engine that owns objects, calls back into Jet during `step`, and uses virtual methods; the
callback captures local state because its retention fact is call-scoped; a Jet type implements a C++ interface.

```cpp
// physics.hpp (a third-party library, binary plus header)
class ContactListener { public: virtual void on_contact(int a, int b, float impulse) = 0; virtual ~ContactListener(); };
class World {
public:
    World(float gravity);
    int add_box(float x, float y, float w, float h);
    void step(float dt, ContactListener& listener);     // calls listener during step only
    struct Pos { float x, y; };
    Pos position(int body) const;
};
```

```jet
package: {
    name: "sim"
    deps: { phys: cpp@"vendor/physics" }   // a local directory: library plus header
}

use phys: cpp.physics
use cpp.physics.[ContactListener]

Hit: struct {
    a: I32
    b: I32
}

Recorder: struct {
    hits: [Hit]
    strongest: F32
}

// A Jet struct implements a C++ abstract class; cppbind writes the vtable shim.
impl Recorder.ContactListener {
    on_contact: fn(&self, a: I32, b: I32, impulse: F32) {
        self.hits.push(Hit{a: a, b: b})
        if impulse > self.strongest -> self.strongest = impulse
    }
}

run: fn() {
    world := phys.World.new(-9.8)
    _ground: world.add_box(0.0, 0.0, 100.0, 1.0)
    crate: world.add_box(0.0, 10.0, 1.0, 1.0)
    rec := Recorder{hits: [], strongest: 0.0}
    loop frame in 0..<120 {
        world.step(1.0 / 60.0, rec)                  // listener is call-scoped: no retention, no Send needed
    }
    print("contacts: {rec.hits.len()}")
    print("strongest: {rec.strongest}")
    print("crate at y = {world.position(crate).y}")
}
```

```text
contacts: 94
strongest: 6.53
crate at y = 1.0
```

If `on_contact` panicked, the stop would show Jet and C++ frames in one list and would not unwind through
`World::step`. The binary is in-process, so `jet audit foreign` lists it as trusted code. The callback type is infallible, so a Jet listener that could fail must handle its failure inside.

### Best competitor equivalent

```rust
// Rust + cxx: a bridge module listing every type and method; virtual callbacks need a hand-written C++ subclass.
#[cxx::bridge]
mod ffi {
    unsafe extern "C++" {
        include!("physics.hpp");
        type World;
        fn new_world(gravity: f32) -> UniquePtr<World>;
        fn add_box(self: Pin<&mut World>, x: f32, y: f32, w: f32, h: f32) -> i32;
    }
}
// Swift 5.9 C++ interop imports classes directly, but virtual methods cannot be overridden from Swift.
```

Three complete Jet libraries and the host code that calls them. `#Export(hosts…)` feeds one export table; each
host's `Language` package (`cbind`, `pybind`, `jsbind`) writes that host's files through `Language.export`
(D-FFI-EXPORT3). Today `#Export(c)` already exports primitives and `Text` with a generated header and
`jet_text_free`; everything beyond that is PROPOSED. Outputs are design targets.

## Simple: scalars to C

```jet
// mathx.jet
#Export(c)
clamp: fn(x: F64, lo: F64, hi: F64) -> F64 {
    if x < lo -> lo else if x > hi -> hi else -> x
}

#Export(c)
greet: fn(name: Text) -> Text { "hello, {name}" }
```

```sh
$ jet build --lib --host c mathx.jet
  out/c/libmathx.a  out/c/mathx.h
```

```c
// main.c
#include "mathx.h"
#include <stdio.h>
int main(void) {
    printf("%f\n", mathx_clamp(12.0, 0.0, 10.0));
    jet_text t = mathx_greet(jet_text_from("Ada"));
    printf("%.*s\n", (int)t.len, t.ptr);
    jet_text_free(t);
}
```

```text
10.000000
hello, Ada
```

## Medium: records and errors to C and Python

```jet
// geo.jet
#Export(c, py)
Point: struct {
    lat: F64
    lon: F64
}

#Export(c, py)
#Error enum GeoError { OutOfRange(F64), Empty }

#Export(c, py)
centroid: fn(points: [Point]) -> Point !GeoError {
    if points.len() == 0 -> return Err(.Empty)
    loop p in points {
        if p.lat < -90.0 || p.lat > 90.0 -> return Err(.OutOfRange(p.lat))
    }
    n: Float.from_int(points.len())
    Point{lat: points.map((p) -> p.lat).sum() / n, lon: points.map((p) -> p.lon).sum() / n}
}
```

```c
// generated geo.h (excerpt)
typedef struct { double lat; double lon; } geo_Point;
typedef struct { int32_t kind; double out_of_range; } geo_GeoError;   // kind: GEO_GEOERROR_OUT_OF_RANGE, GEO_GEOERROR_EMPTY
int32_t geo_centroid(const geo_Point *points, size_t count, geo_Point *out, geo_GeoError *err);   // 0 = ok
```

```python
# app.py, using the generated wheel
import geo
print(geo.centroid([geo.Point(lat=48.8, lon=2.3), geo.Point(lat=52.5, lon=13.4)]))
try:
    geo.centroid([])
except geo.GeoError.Empty:
    print("no points")
```

```text
Point(lat=50.65, lon=7.85)
no points
```

## Complex: callbacks and async to C, Python and JavaScript

```jet
// crawler.jet
use http: core.http.client
use net: core.net

#Export(c, py, js)
Page: struct {
    url: String
    status: Int
    bytes: Int
}

// A call-scoped callback: hosts may pass any function or closure.
#Export(c, py, js)
crawl: fn(start: String, limit: Int, on_page: fn(Page)) -> Int !net.HTTPError {
    seen := 0
    queue := [start]
    loop {
        if seen >= limit || queue.len() == 0 -> break
        url: queue.pop_front() ?? break
        resp: http.get(url)
        on_page(Page{url: url, status: resp.status, bytes: resp.body.len()})
        loop link in resp.links() -> queue.push(link)
        seen += 1
    }
    seen
}

// A waiting function: completion callback plus cancel handle in C, async def in Python, Promise in JS.
#Export(c, py, js)
fetch_title: fn(url: String) -> String !net.HTTPError {
    http.get(url).html().title() ?? "(untitled)"
}
```

```c
// C host: the completion-callback idiom C programmers know from libuv and libcurl.
static void on_page(void *ctx, const crawler_Page *p) { printf("%d %s\n", (int)p->status, p->url.ptr); }
static void on_title(void *ctx, jet_text title, const crawler_HTTPError *err) {
    if (err) { fprintf(stderr, "failed: %d\n", err->kind); return; }
    printf("title: %.*s\n", (int)title.len, title.ptr);
    jet_text_free(title);
}
int main(void) {
    crawler_HTTPError err;
    int64_t n;
    crawler_crawl(jet_text_from("https://jet-lang.dev"), 5, on_page, NULL, &n, &err);
    crawler_cancel c = crawler_fetch_title(jet_text_from("https://jet-lang.dev"), on_title, NULL);
    crawler_run_until_idle();          // or integrate crawler_poll_fd() into the host's event loop
}
```

```python
# Python host
import asyncio, crawler
crawler.crawl("https://jet-lang.dev", 5, lambda p: print(p.status, p.url))
print(asyncio.run(crawler.fetch_title("https://jet-lang.dev")))
```

```js
// JavaScript host
import { crawl, fetchTitle } from "./crawler.js";
crawl("https://jet-lang.dev", 5, (p) => console.log(p.status, p.url));
console.log(await fetchTitle("https://jet-lang.dev"));
```

```text
200 https://jet-lang.dev
200 https://jet-lang.dev/docs
...
title: Jet
```

### Best competitor equivalent

```rust
// Rust: three tools. cbindgen for C (no async), PyO3 + pyo3-asyncio for Python, wasm-bindgen for JS.
#[no_mangle] pub extern "C" fn crawler_crawl(start: *const c_char, limit: i64,
    cb: extern "C" fn(*mut c_void, *const Page), ctx: *mut c_void) -> i64 { ... }
#[pyfunction] fn crawl(py: Python<'_>, start: String, limit: i64, on_page: PyObject) -> PyResult<i64> { ... }
#[wasm_bindgen] pub async fn fetch_title(url: String) -> Result<String, JsValue> { ... }
```

Three complete programs that call Rust through `use rust.<crate>`. The `rustbind` package (root `rust`) reads the
crate's public API from rustdoc JSON with a pinned toolchain, writes a `#[no_mangle]` shim crate for the items the
program uses, and emits Jet types. Rust's own types are facts with the strongest evidence: `&T` is non-null and
borrowed, `String` and `Box` are owned, `Result` is an error, `Send` and `Sync` are thread facts, `Drop` is
`Close`. Public crates arrive prebuilt from the store (D-FFI-TOOLS1). Outputs are design targets.

## Simple: regex

```jet
package: {
    name: "emails"
    deps: { regex: rust@"regex@1.11" }
}

use re: rust.regex

run: fn() {
    pattern: re.Regex.new("^[a-z.]+@[a-z]+\\.[a-z]{2,}$")      // Result<Regex, regex::Error> -> Regex !re.Error
    loop s in ["ann@example.com", "not an email", "bo.li@jet.dev"] {
        print("{s}: {pattern.is_match(s)}")                        // &str borrows the Jet text: zero copy
    }
}
```

```text
ann@example.com: true
not an email: false
bo.li@jet.dev: true
```

## Medium: serde_json records

What it shows: a Rust generic function instantiated for a Jet `#Codable` struct by deriving the matching serde
impls in the shim; Rust errors as Jet errors; an iterator as a Jet loop source.

```jet
package: {
    name: "orders"
    deps: { serde_json: rust@"serde_json@1.0" }
}

use sj: rust.serde_json
use fs: core.files

#Codable
Line: struct {
    sku: String
    qty: Int
}

#Codable
Order: struct {
    id: String
    lines: [Line]
}

run: fn() {
    text: fs.read("orders.json")
    orders: sj.from_str<[Order]>(text)              // the shim derives Deserialize for Order's layout
    loop o in orders {
        total: o.lines.map((l) -> l.qty).sum()
        print("{o.id}: {total} items")
    }
    print(sj.to_string_pretty(orders[0]))
    broken: sj.from_str<Order>("{\"id\": 5}")         // serde_json::Error -> sj.Error, with line and column
    print(broken.id)
}
```

```text
A-100: 7 items
B-200: 2 items
{
  "id": "A-100",
  ...
}
Error [sj.Error]: invalid type: integer `5`, expected a string at line 1 column 9
  in Rust  serde_json-1.0.133/src/de.rs   serde_json::from_str::<Order>
  -- crossing: rust.serde_json from_str  (binder rustbind 1.2.0, rule emit_generic) --
  in Jet   orders.jet:28:15  run()
```

## Complex: a crate that runs its own threads (tokio-backed)

What it shows: an async Rust API whose futures become Jet wait points; a `Send + 'static` callback that the crate
calls from its own worker threads, delivered on the owning task; `Drop` as `Close`.

```jet
package: {
    name: "feeds"
    deps: { feedkit: rust@"feedkit@0.4" }       // a tokio-based RSS client
}

use fk: rust.feedkit

Counter: struct {
    seen: Int
}

run: fn() {
    client: fk.Client.builder().timeout(5s).build()         // owns a tokio runtime; Drop -> Close at scope end
    feeds: ["https://blog.rust-lang.org/feed.xml", "https://jet-lang.dev/feed.xml"]

    // async fn fetch(&self, url: &str) -> Result<Feed, fk::Error> becomes a wait point.
    tasks: feeds.map((u) -> task client.fetch(u))
    loop t in tasks {
        feed: t.join()
        print("{feed.title}: {feed.items.len()} items")
    }

    // fn watch(&self, url: &str, f: impl Fn(Item) + Send + 'static) -> WatchHandle
    // Retention: 'static (until the handle drops). Thread: the crate's workers. Delivery: this task.
    seen: shared Counter{seen: 0}
    _watch: client.watch(feeds[1], (item) -> {
        print("new: {item.title}")
        seen.seen += 1                                         // Shared<Counter>: safe from any thread
    }, deliver: .Owner)
    sleep(200ms)
    print("seen {seen.seen}")
}                                                              // the watch closes first, then client
```

```text
Rust Blog: 10 items
Jet: 4 items
new: FFI v3 lands
seen 1
```

`watch` retains the closure, so its captures must be owned or sendable. A plain mutable local would be refused:

```text
Error [E3233]: `watch` keeps this callback after the call returns, so it cannot borrow `counter`.
 Fix: Make it shared: `counter: shared Counter{seen: 0}`.
```

### Best competitor equivalent

```rust
// The closest cross-language equivalent: exposing the same async crate to Python with PyO3, by hand.
#[pyfunction]
fn fetch(py: Python<'_>, url: String) -> PyResult<Bound<'_, PyAny>> {
    pyo3_asyncio::tokio::future_into_py(py, async move { Ok(client().fetch(&url).await?.title) })
}
// The binding author writes this per function; Jet's rustbind writes it from rustdoc JSON.
```

Three complete programs for the `wasm32-web` target that call JavaScript through `use js.<package>`. The `jsbind`
package (root `js`) reads the package's `.d.ts` with a pinned TypeScript compiler, emits Jet types and writes the
JS glue the page loads. TypeScript types are the facts: `string | null` is `?String`, `Promise<T>` is a wait
point, a thrown `Error` is a Jet error when the `.d.ts` or a pack declares it. Browser effects follow the spec's
browser rules. Outputs are design targets.

## Simple: date-fns

```jet
package: {
    name: "dates"
    deps: { date_fns: js@"date-fns@4.1" }
    targets: [wasm32-web]
}

use dates: js.date_fns

run: fn() {
    now: dates.Date.now()
    print(dates.format(now, "yyyy-MM-dd"))
    print(dates.format_distance(dates.sub_days(now, 3), now))     // camelCase becomes snake_case
    print(dates.is_weekend(now))
}
```

```text
2026-10-31
3 days
true
```

## Medium: chart.js with fetch

What it shows: a canvas element from the page, a JS class constructed from Jet, a Jet record passed as a JS object
literal by the `.d.ts` interface, and `fetch` as a wait point.

```jet
package: {
    name: "dashboard"
    deps: { chart: js@"chart.js@4.4" }
    targets: [wasm32-web]
}

use chart: js.chart_js
use web: core.web

#Codable
Point: struct {
    day: String
    visits: Int
}

run: fn() !web.FetchError {
    points: web.fetch("/api/visits").json<[Point]>()        // Promise -> wait point
    canvas: web.document.query<web.Canvas>("#visits")
    _c: chart.Chart.new(canvas, chart.ChartConfiguration{
        type_: .Line,                                           // the .d.ts field `type`, renamed by jsbind
        data: chart.ChartData{
            labels: points.map((p) -> p.day),
            datasets: [chart.Dataset{label: "Visits", data: points.map((p) -> p.visits)}],
        },
    })                                                          // destroy() at scope end via Close
    web.wait_for_unload()
}
```

The page shows a line chart of visits per day.

## Complex: a web component written in Jet, used from JS, using JS

What it shows: exporting a Jet type as a custom element (D-FFI-EXPORT3, host `js`), JS events delivered to Jet
callbacks, a JS library (`marked`) called from Jet, and a JS caller awaiting a Jet waiting function.

```jet
package: {
    name: "md_editor"
    deps: {
        marked: js@"marked@14.1"
        purify: js@"dompurify@3.2"
    }
    targets: [wasm32-web]
}

use md: js.marked
use purify: js.dompurify
use web: core.web

#Export(js)
MarkdownEditor: struct {
    area: web.TextArea
    preview: web.Element
}

render_into: fn(preview: web.Element, text: String) {
    html: md.parse(text, md.Options{gfm: true})                     // marked returns a string
    preview.set_inner_html(purify.sanitize(html))                     // never insert unsanitized HTML
}

#Export(js)
impl MarkdownEditor {
    mount: fn(root: web.Element) -> MarkdownEditor {
        area: web.document.create<web.TextArea>("textarea")
        preview: web.document.create<web.Element>("div")
        root.append(area)
        root.append(preview)
        area.on_input((e) -> render_into(preview, e.target.value))     // retained; captures a JS handle, which is sendable
        MarkdownEditor{area: area, preview: preview}
    }

    render: fn(self, text: String) {
        self.area.set_value(text)                                       // a method on the JS handle
        render_into(self.preview, text)
    }

    // A waiting Jet function becomes a Promise for JS callers.
    save: fn(self, url: String) -> Int !web.FetchError {
        web.fetch(url, method: .Post, body: self.area.value).status
    }
}
